.TH sattrack 1
.SH NAME
sattrack \- calculates the position of satellite in orbit at a given time, optionally relative to an observer on the ground
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sattrack
[OPTION...]
[FILE]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B sattrack
utility reads a TLE file, then calculates the position of the satellite at a given time, or
at a series of times. Optionally, the location of an observer on the ground can be given, in
which case 
.B sattrack
will also calculate various aspects of the satellite's visibility to that observer.

.SH OPTIONS
The options are:

.TP
.BR \-h,\-\-help
Show short help and exit

.TP
.BR \-V,\-\-version
Show the version and exit

.TP
.BR \-v,\-\-verbose
Show debug output. Not needed during normal usage

.TP
.BR \-l,\-\-location=LOCATION
Set the location of an observer. The location is set as a string consisting of latitude and longitude,
separated with a comma and without whitespace. Both latitude and longitude are signed decimals, with a 
range of -90.0 to 90.0 for latitude, and -180.0 to 180.0 for longitude. As an example, the option for
location Amsterdam would be 
.B \-\-location=52.3667,4.8833
and for Buenos Aires
.B \-\-location=\-34.5997,\-58.3819
\&. Setting a location is optional, but certain output-fields are not available without a location

.TP
.BR \-s,\-\-start=TIMESTAMP
Set the time for which to calculate the satellite's position. 
.B TIMESTAMP
is formatted as yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ssZ and is in UTC. The default is to use the current date and time. Without a
.B \-\-count
option, only the position at 
.B TIMESTAMP
is calculated, but with a 
.B \-\-count
option, the position is calculated for a series of timestamps starting at
.B TIMESTAMP

.TP
.BR \-c,\-\-count=COUNT
Set the number of consecutive timestamps for which to calculate the satellite's position. Defaults to 
.B 1
.\&.

.TP
.BR \-i,\-\-interval=INTERVAL
Set the interval in seconds between consecutive calculations of the satellite's position. For example, with
options
.B \-\-count=3
and
.B \-\-interval=60
the satellite's position will be calculated at the timestamp set with
.B \-\-start
and 1 and 2 minutes after that.

.TP
.BR \-n,\-\-satellite\-name=NAME
In case the given TLE file contains multiple satellites, specify the name of the satellite to use. Default
behaviour is to use the first satellite from the TLE file.

.TP
.BR \-f,\-\-format=rows|cols
Set the output-format to either
.B rows
(which is easier readable for a human user) or 
.B cols
(which is easier to process by another program). Defaults to
.B rows
when 
.B \-\-count
is 
.B 1
, otherwise 
.B cols
\&.

.TP
.BR \-H,\-\-headers
When the output format is
.B cols
, print a row with column-headers first.

.TP
.BR \-F,\-\-fields=FIELDS
Sets the fields to print for each calculated satellite-position. 
.B FIELDS
is a character-string with each characters specifying a field. See the
.B OUTPUT FIELDS
section for a listing of all fields. Defaults to 
.B trezoaA
when a 
.B \-\-location
option is set, or to
.B toaA
when it isn't.

.SH ARGUMENTS
The 
.B FILE
argument specifies the TLE file. The special value
.B \-
means to use 
.B stdin
\&. Without a
.B FILE
argument, 
.B sattrack
uses the contents of the 
.B ${ORBIT_TOOLS_TLE} 
environment variable.

.SH OUTPUT FIELDS
The \fBFIELDS\fR string to the \fB\-\-fields\fR option may contain the following characters:
.TP
.BR t
The date and time, formatted as \fByyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ssZ\fR

.TP
.BR T
The date and time, in seconds since the UNIX epoch

.TP
.BR r
The range (i.e. the distance between the satellite and the observer) in km. Only available
when \fB\-\-location\fR is set

.TP
.BR l
The elevation (i.e. the angle from the horizon at which the satellite is seen by the observer) in
degrees. Only available when \fB\-\-location\fR is set

.TP
.BR z
The azimuth (i.e. the compass point at which the satellite is seen by the observer) in degrees. 
Only available when \fB\-\-location\fR is set

.TP
.BR o
The longitude of the sub-satellite point (i.e. the point at the earth surface directly underneath the
satellite), in degrees

.TP
.BR a
The latitude of the sub-satellite point, in degrees

.TP
.BR A
The altitude of the satellite, in km

.TP
.BR XYZ
The X, Y and Z coordinate of the satellite's position in the ECI coordinate system, in km

.TP
.BR 123
The X, Y and Z component of the satellite's velocity in the ECI coordinate system, in km/s

.TP
.BR g
The satellite's ground-track velocity (i.e. the velocity at which the SSP moves over the
earth surface), in km/s

.TP
.BR G
The satellite's ground-track direction (i.e. the direction in which the SSP moves over the
earth surface) as a compas point in degrees


.SH EXIT CODES
The \fBls300commander\fR utility will exit with \fI0\fR on success, or an exit code \fI> 0\fR if an error occurs.
